Collision & Collision Resolution Techniques
-------------------------------------------
What is Collision?
When two keys produce same index.
Ex: h(x) = x (mod 5) 
For:
Key: 10 -> Hash Value: 0
Key: 15 -> Hash Value: 0
Both go to index 0.This is called Collision.

Consider a = [15, 10, 12, 20], size is 5
h(15) = 15 (mod 5)-> 0
h(10) = 10 (mod 5)-> 0-
  > In 0 index already 15 available
  -> So it is called as collision

Table Visual:
0 | 15 |
1 |    |
2 |    |
3 |    |
4 |    |

Collision Handling Techniques
-----------------------------
Two main techniques:
    1)Chaining
    2)Open Addressing

1)Chaining
----------
Hash Function: h(k) = k (mod m)
Example: m = 5, Keys: 10, 15, 20, 12
Table:
| Index | Table |
| :---: | :---  |
| 0     | [10] -> [15] -> [20] |
| 1 | — |
| 2 | [12] |
| 3 | — |
| 4 | — |
How it works: All keys that hash to the same index are stored in a linked list.

- in index of 0 have 3 values , so every values have key:value.
using hash(value)-> key generated.
eg. If you store 10 use:- hash(10)

-So this procss called Chaining technique.

2. Open Addressing
-------------------
2.1 Linear Probing
-------------------
-If the collision occur then it can't create linked list in a specific index.
-It find next index free or not if free the store the data .

Example: m = 5, Keys: 10, 15, 20, 12
Table:
| Index | Table |
| :---: | :---: |
| 0     | 10    |
| 1     | 15    |
| 2     | 12    |
| 3     | 20    |
| 4     | —     |
